# Keystone specification layer — the CONTENT pin.
#
# WHICH FIELD IS THE PIN: the `_digest` values. They are the citable anchor.
# A consumer cites `H1…H9 @ <digest>`, exactly as a conformance number is cited at
# `core_executed_check_set_digest`, and for the same reason: published commits are
# authored fresh at the release boundary ([ADR-0012] Am. 1), so an internal SHA
# resolves to nothing for a reader outside this ecosystem. We have already lost four
# oracle pins to that, and the content digest is what carried the verdict across their
# death.
#
# The digest is `sha256` over the WHOLE FILE. The document does not contain its own
# digest — that is why it can be hashed whole, and why the pin lives here rather than in
# a header the document would have to keep in step with itself.
#
# AMENDMENT DISCIPLINE, mirroring spec-data/: an H-number is append-only and a
# requirement is never edited in place once a peer has been measured against it. A
# withdrawn requirement is marked withdrawn and KEEPS ITS NUMBER. Any change to the
# normative body moves BOTH the version and the digest, and the superseded digest is
# recorded below as `retired_*` so a consumer citing the old one can tell that it moved
# rather than finding a number that resolves to nothing.
#
# Verified by tools/keystone-spec-gate.py, in `make lint`.

keystone_peer_version = 1.0
keystone_peer_digest  = 62e1a1dd156bfd3e7760a86bf4b75b0433bd5e234d37fd5bc79c5823c3cd938a
keystone_peer_path    = docs/spec/SPEC-KEYSTONE-PEER.md

# Requirements in force at this digest. H-numbers are append-only; this list only grows.
keystone_peer_requirements = H1 H2 H3 H4 H5 H6 H7 H8 H9
keystone_peer_withdrawn    =

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Superseded digests. Never delete a row; a consumer may be citing one.
# (none yet — 1.0 is the first published version)
